From: Jacob Cook <jacobmcook1984@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: rk817-charger: take the ocv floor when the
 saved soc is below it too

Off the charger at boot, a counter a tenth of the pack below the ocv
floor is rejected in favour of the saved soc, but the saved soc was
never checked against that floor. When the pack charged while the
system was powered off and neither the counter nor the save saw it,
both are low and the stale save stands. An RG DS Plus left powered off
on a charger for 22 hours, then unplugged and started, read 25% with
4.255V on the pack (about 95% on the table) and stayed there.

When the saved soc is a tenth of the pack below the floor as well, take
the floor. Under load the terminal voltage sits below the open circuit
voltage, so the floor still reads low; the next rested relax pair
corrects the rest.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
--- a/drivers/power/supply/rk817_charger.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/rk817_charger.c
@@ -941,7 +941,20 @@
 			else
 				keep_saved = boot_soc < table_soc - 10000;
 
-			if (keep_saved)
+			if (keep_saved && !charger->plugged_in &&
+			    charger->soc < table_soc - 10000) {
+				/*
+				 * The saved soc is below the floor as well: the
+				 * pack charged while the system was off and neither
+				 * the counter nor the save saw it (an RG DS Plus
+				 * powered off on the charger for 22 hours booted at
+				 * 25% with 4.255V on the pack). Take the floor.
+				 */
+				dev_info(charger->dev,
+					 "counter %d and saved soc %d below the voltage floor %d, taking the floor\n",
+					 boot_soc, charger->soc, table_soc);
+				charger->soc = table_soc;
+			} else if (keep_saved)
 				dev_info(charger->dev,
 					 "counter %d, voltage allows %d, keeping saved soc %d\n",
 					 boot_soc, table_soc, charger->soc);
